## 🎯 THE 5 CRITICAL CHANGES FOR 2026

### 1. **Signal Forms = New Standard (Not Reactive Forms)**

**What Changed:**
- Old: FormBuilder, FormControl, FormGroup
- New: `model()` directive + signals for type-safe two-way binding

**Why It Matters:**
- 60% less form code
- Zero boilerplate setup
- Compile-time type safety
- Automatic integration with zoneless change detection
- Every form should use this pattern now

**Example:**
```typescript
// Old (Deprecated 2026):
form = this.fb.group({
  firstName: ['', Validators.required],
  lastName: ['', Validators.required]
});

// New (2026 Standard):
user = model<User>({ firstName: '', lastName: '' });
isValid = computed(() => user().firstName.length > 0);
// Template: <input [(ngModel)]="user().firstName">
```

**Interview Impact:** If you mention Reactive Forms in 2026, expect a follow-up: "Have you considered Signal Forms?" Always lead with Signal Forms.

---

### 2. **Zoneless Change Detection is Default (Zone.js Deprecated)**

**What Changed:**
- Old: Zone.js intercepts EVERY async operation
- New: Only signals notify when to run change detection

**Why It Matters:**
- 60-80% fewer change detection cycles
- Better performance without workarounds
- No need for `.detectChanges()`, `markForCheck()`, etc.
- Works perfectly with signals and OnPush

**Real Numbers:**
```
Old (Zone.js): 100+ change detection cycles in 3-minute user session
New (Zoneless): 5-10 precise change detection cycles

Result: 60-80% reduction in CPU usage and re-renders
```

**Interview Impact:** Always mention "zoneless is default in Angular 21" when discussing change detection. This signals you're current.

---

### 3. **Vitest Replaces Karma (Test Runner)**

**What Changed:**
- Old: Karma (slow, complex config, browser-based)
- New: Vitest (fast, simple config, Node-based)

**Why It Matters:**
- 10x faster test execution
- ESM-first (modern JavaScript)
- Minimal configuration
- TestBed API stays identical (easy migration)

**Interview Impact:** If you mention Karma as the default test runner, expect: "Have you considered migrating to Vitest?" Always mention Vitest for new projects.

---

### 4. **@defer Blocks for Lazy Loading (Stable Feature)**

**What Changed:**
- Old: `*ngIf` (creates component, hides it)
- New: `@defer` (doesn't create until needed)

**Why It Matters:**
- True code splitting (component not bundled until needed)
- Smaller initial bundle
- Better Time-to-Interactive (LCP, FID)
- Automatic lazy loading for heavy components

**Example:**
```typescript
// Old:
<div *ngIf="showChart">
  <app-heavy-chart></app-heavy-chart> <!-- Created but hidden -->
</div>

// New:
@defer (on viewport) {
  <app-heavy-chart></app-heavy-chart> <!-- Only created when scrolled -->
} @placeholder {
  <p>Loading...</p>
}
```

**Interview Impact:** Show you understand lazy loading benefits. Mention @defer when discussing bundle optimization.

---

### 5. **Modern Control Flow (@if, @for, @switch Standard)**

**What Changed:**
- Old: `*ngIf`, `*ngFor`, `*ngSwitch` (directive syntax)
- New: `@if`, `@for`, `@switch` (JavaScript-like syntax)

**Why It Matters:**
- Cleaner, more readable templates
- Compile-time safety (fails at build if wrong)
- Better performance (generates more efficient code)
- Mandatory `track` in `@for` prevents performance bugs

**Interview Impact:** Write templates using new syntax. If you write `*ngIf`, you'll get a note: "Could use modern control flow here."

